      "math_academy_id": "e-4079",
      "problem": "By expressing $\\dfrac{1}{2}\\log_3{x}$ in the form $\\log_3 \\left(x^k\\right),$ determine the value of $k.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's recall the power rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\log_n(c^m)= m\\log_n\\left( c \\right)\n$$\n\nUsing the power rule for logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{3}⁡x & ={log}_{3}⁡({x}^{1/2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k = \\dfrac{1}{2}.$",
